course: dictionary
lesson: 15710
title: Totalled: added up or wrecked
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Totalled» comes from «total», which means the final amount when you add everything together. «The bill totalled ten pounds» means that when all the items were added up, the answer was ten pounds.
explain: In a maths problem, «The sales totalled 120» tells you that all the sales added together make 120. Look at the numbers around the word, because they are the clues. You can check by adding them yourself.
explain: Sometimes «totalled» is slang. «He totalled his car» does not mean he added up numbers. It means the car was smashed so badly in a crash that it cannot be fixed. Read the rest of the sentence to see which meaning fits.
fact: Total means to add up numbers to find the final amount.
fact: «Totalled» is the past tense of «total»: «The bill totalled ten pounds» means it added up to ten pounds.
fact: In American English the spelling is «totaled», with one l.
fact: In informal slang, a «totalled» car is completely wrecked and cannot be repaired.
fact: Numbers in the sentence are a clue that «totalled» means added up; a crash or damage is a clue that it means wrecked.
exercise: «Ali added the scores, and they totalled 45.» What does totalled mean here? | Crashed into 45 | *Added up to 45 | Were written in 45 lines | Were cut into 45 pieces
exercise: «After the crash, Sam said, "My bike is totalled."» What does totalled mean? | *Smashed so badly it cannot be fixed | Added up to a big number | Painted in bright colours
exercise: «The shopping bill totalled twenty pounds.» Which numbers or words are the clues that totalled means added up? | Bill | *Twenty pounds | All of these are clues | Shopping
test: «Mia totalled her spending: 4 + 6 + 10 = 20.» What does totalled mean here? | *Added up to 20 | Spent all her money | Divided into three parts | Lost count
test: «Dad said the car was totalled after the crash.» What does totalled mean? | Added up all the costs | *Wrecked beyond repair | Cleaned and polished | Found in a shop
exam: Which sentence uses totalled to mean wrecked? | The sums totalled 50 | She totalled the scores at the end | The receipt totalled £12 | *The lorry was totalled in the crash
